 "api_error_status": null,
 "result": "Bundle rules are recorded when you declare them and replayed onto the app at registration.\n\n1. **Record at declaration:** `Bundle.attach_route_rule` (`src/tessel/sansio/bundles.py:413`) doesn't touch the app. It validates that the endpoint and view name have no dots, then calls `self.record(lambda s: s.attach_route_rule(...))`. `Bundle.record` (`:224`) appends that lambda to `self.deferred_functions`.\n2. **Register the bundle:**\n   - `App.register_bundle` (`src/tessel/sansio/app.py:571`) calls `Bundle.register(app, options)` (`sansio/bundles.py:273`).\n   - `register` builds a `BundleSetupState` via `make_setup_state` (`:246`).\n   - If the bundle has a static folder, it adds the static route through `state.attach_route_rule`.\n   - It then loops `for deferred in self.deferred_functions: deferred(state)` (`:334`).\n3. **Apply to the app:** `BundleSetupState.attach_route_rule` (`sansio/bundles.py:87`) does the work for each replayed rule.\n   - It prepends `url_prefix` to the rule.\n   - It defaults the subdomain.\n   - It merges `url_defaults`.\n   - It prefixes the endpoint with `name_prefix.name.`.\n   - Finally it calls `app.attach_route_rule(...)` (`sansio/app.py:606`), which adds the rule to the URL map.\n\n`src/tessel/bundles.py` only defines the concrete `Bundle` subclass and re-exports `BundleSetupState`.",
